      "id": "LITELLM-2026-10-21",
      "image": "docker.io/litellm/litellm",
      "digests": ["sha256:1bbcb1443bc962e45f5aad53fe3d2c30600e0379715fb6c664cdd3ac126c339e"],
      "tags": ["v1.107.0", "latest", "main-latest", "main-stable"],
      "url": "https://docs.litellm.ai/blog/security-incident-oct-21-2026",
      "note": "The post names the Docker Hub tags and this image index digest (compromised from 11:46:56 to 14:20 UTC on 2026-10-21) without the repository; docker.io/litellm/litellm carries those four tags (v1.107.0 removed, the rest repointed), and docker.io/berriai/litellm does not exist (404 on 2026-10-28). The post's GHCR digests are truncated, so none is listed. A digest is matched whatever registry name a reference gives it, since a digest names content; the tags are listed for the record and never matched."
    },
    {
      "id": "ARTEX-2026-11-05-TOMICDD",
      "image": "docker.io/tomicdd/artex",
      "all_tags": true,
      "digests": [],
      "tags": [],
      "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/backdoored-docker-image-for-artex-ai.html",
      "note": "The Hacker News (5 Nov 2026): this Docker Hub image of the ARTEX AI console, downloaded 9,000 times, adds a hidden verifyCaptcha() to the login handler, which runs the rest of a CAPTCHA value that starts with a secret marker through sh -c as root before any credential check. 62 ARTEX consoles served the backdoored build between 2 and 4 Nov 2026, and a modified GitHub copy of ARTEX changed one docker-compose.yml line to pull this image. The article names no tag and no digest, so every reference to the repository is matched."
    },
